WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.113
Here is a charge of 1999 cents.

2
00:00:05.113 --> 00:00:09.860
You hand it to a small block of code that doubles the amount.

3
00:00:10.067 --> 00:00:14.713
When the block is done, the call gives back one of two things.

4
00:00:14.713 --> 00:00:16.586
The block's result, 3998.

5
00:00:16.586 --> 00:00:18.684
Or the charge you handed in.

6
00:00:19.467 --> 00:00:23.052
Inside the block, the charge shows up in one of two ways.

7
00:00:23.052 --> 00:00:27.330
As this, the block runs inside the charge, so you write amountMinor.

8
00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:31.796
As it, the charge comes in as a parameter, so you write it.amountMinor.

9
00:00:31.967 --> 00:00:34.005
Two questions, two answers each.

10
00:00:34.005 --> 00:00:38.273
That makes four cells, and Kotlin's five scope functions fill them.

11
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.255
Same block, five functions.

12
00:00:40.255 --> 00:00:42.140
What does each one give back?

13
00:00:44.533 --> 00:00:47.414
let, run and with give back 3998.

14
00:00:47.414 --> 00:00:50.557
also and apply give back the charge.

15
00:00:50.557 --> 00:00:53.001
Java has no scope functions.

16
00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.654
Each cell has a typical job.

17
00:00:55.654 --> 00:00:59.772
apply configures an object, and hands the same object back.

18
00:00:59.967 --> 00:01:05.179
also does a side step, like a log line, and the object flows on unchanged.

19
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:09.467
let, after a safe call, runs only when the value is not null.

20
00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:11.600
A missing token skips the block.

21
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:16.500
run and with compute something new from the object's members, like a risk score.

22
00:01:17.433 --> 00:01:18.302
Now the trap.

23
00:01:18.302 --> 00:01:23.249
It reads like if, else: post the cached payment, or fetch one and post it.

24
00:01:23.467 --> 00:01:28.977
First call: the ledger is empty, post returns an entry id, and run is skipped.

25
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:30.424
Second call.

26
00:01:30.424 --> 00:01:34.959
The payment is still cached, but the ledger has posted it already.

27
00:01:34.959 --> 00:01:36.127
Which blocks run?

28
00:01:40.167 --> 00:01:42.389
post returns null for a duplicate.

29
00:01:42.389 --> 00:01:45.461
The Elvis operator tests the value on its left.

30
00:01:45.461 --> 00:01:49.383
That is the result of the let block, not the cached payment.

31
00:01:49.383 --> 00:01:51.213
It is null, so run runs too.

32
00:01:51.213 --> 00:01:55.069
A cache miss, a fetch from the database, and a second post.

33
00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.679
Both blocks ran.

34
00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.671
Java's Optional has the same trap.

35
00:01:59.671 --> 00:02:04.909
Write the branch you mean with if or when, or pick the payment first and post it once.

36
00:02:07.233 --> 00:02:12.317
So: what should the call give back, and does the block see the object as this or as it?

37
00:02:12.533 --> 00:02:13.576
A quick check.

38
00:02:13.576 --> 00:02:15.512
The payment is authorized.

39
00:02:15.512 --> 00:02:17.225
What status comes back?

40
00:02:19.633 --> 00:02:20.420
Authorized.

41
00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:24.212
also gives back the payment, and the copy is dropped.
